From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikel Astiz <mastiz@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 04:37:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] [sync] Enforce Nigori key continuity for inline keybag
 updates

Previously, key continuity across Trusted Vault key rotations or
transitions (e.g., to CUSTOM_PASSPHRASE) was only enforced when
decrypting pending_keys via external keys, using a dedicated
last_default_trusted_vault_key_name field in NigoriState. When a new
encryption_keybag could be decrypted inline in UpdateLocalState()
because the local cryptographer already held the decrypting key,
TryDecryptPendingKeysWith() cleared last_default_trusted_vault_key_name
without enforcing key continuity or restoring it, allowing a subsequent
passphrase transition to bypass key continuity.

To fix this, NigoriSyncBridgeImpl::TryDecryptPendingKeysWith() now
queries the prior default key directly from CryptographerImpl via a
temporary GetDefaultKeyNamePossiblyInvalidated() getter (which returns
default_encryption_key_name_ even while pending_keys are present) and
enforces key continuity for all pending-key decryptions (both inline
and external) whenever the prior passphrase type is
TRUSTED_VAULT_PASSPHRASE. Correspondingly,
last_default_trusted_vault_key_name is removed from NigoriState and
NigoriModel, and kNigoriMissingLastTrustedVaultKeyInKeybag is renamed
to kNigoriMissingLastDefaultKeyInKeybag.

CryptographerImpl::IsLocalProtoValid() is also tightened so that a
non-empty key_bag always requires a non-empty default_key_name. No data
migration is included for legacy persisted states with pending_keys and
an empty default_key_name (possible only prior to crrev.com/c/7899678),
as discarding local Nigori state in that rare edge case simply triggers
a harmless re-download from the server.

Bug: 521061749
